Date: Wed, 15 Dec 1993 11:28:37 -0230
From: Bill Garland <bill@kean.ucs.mun.ca>
Subject: META : Privacy 

I'm getting so good at deleting messages after I read them that
I cannot go back to check on who it was who wrote that Village Voice
article, and my question is : is that person a member of the List?

If so, was he or she not violating list rules by discussing list
contents with non-list entities?

If not, where did he or she get all the information? It seems to 
me that some of that article _had_ to come from actually reading
the list, and not just from, say, interviewing an Extropian.

I thought List stuff was supposed to be kept confidential to List
members?

What are the boundaries between spreading the memes and breaching
confidentiality? I mean, sure it is a Good Thing to get the message
out, but all this denigration of Extropians as techno-weenies and
radical fringe anarchists - okay, so what if we _are_ twarfa's,-
but this may not be the exact image we need to have broadcast...
(assuming we have such a need).

Well, whatever, there seems to have been some leakage here. I
assume Extropians want to abide by the welcome-message ppl, and
that List discussions are to be kept private. I realize some members
may be spoofers and/or spies, but we can at least track them down. 

Speaking of spoofers, before I had to cut off Cypherpunks
because of the volume of detweilleristic noise, there was a
mini-flame war there between Ray and Lefty, where Ray stated words
to the effect that Lefty had made certain comments "over on the 
Extropians list for two years" (not an exact quote) - I was going to 
mention this then but didn't, however - isn't the mere fact that Lefty,
who I understand has since left the List, posted something/anything on 
the List, something to be kept private? I can't even remember what they 
were fighting about, and that is not relevant anyway, and I don't want 
to start anything by mentioning it now, but I would like to know that 
the policy about Extropian list privacy is something we all respect.
We always have to assume that all our communications are intercepted
anyway, I suppose, but that is no excuse for loose lips.

