Date: Wed, 15 Dec 93 10:17:23 -0500
From: Jeff Fabijanic <jeff@PROFESSOR-ED.ELF.COM>
Subject: The Importance of Reading

Duncan writes:

>[My (Jeff's) list of often-used non-text info sources]
>
>The baud rate of such exchanges is much lower than reading, however.  


Only if you just consider easily-defined written information. The
amount of social and emotional info I can gather from a short
face-to-face exchange with someone can be far greater than all such
info I would ever be able to gather through that person's writing.
And as a businessman and a generally social primate, such info is
important to me, not just for my personal satisfaction (although I
do enjoy it) but for its survival value as well.

An example. I have been looking for an apartment for the last few
weeks. In the course of this search, I've had to interact with many
real estate agents and house owners, and visit many locations. Now,
a written description of all these locations might have been sufficient
for some preliminary cuts, but there is no way that I can see that I
would have been able to make the best choice without actually seeing
the spaces. The same goes for the agents/owners themselves. There were
several who I quickly realized I could not trust (or simply feel
comfortable with enough to make an unbiased decision). I thanked these
folks for their time and said goodbye. Other agents, though they weren't
able to show me what I wanted right away, obviously had similar aesthetics
and I knew that if I let them continue to show me places, I would find
something I liked. Almost all this information came through non-verbal
channels that would be difficult, if not impossible, to convey through
writing. 


>There is (as yet) no substitute for writing if you want to produce 

>complex, detailed, and abstract communications.  One article on GATT in 

>the Economist contains *much* more information on (and understanding of) 

>GATT than watching CNN and listening to National Commie Radio full time 

>for two years.

I bet fifteen minutes in a room with the GATT delegates would give you
a much better idea whether the thing ever had a chance of going through
than *all* the articles you could ever read. Not all useful information
is encodable in writing.

>The *idea* density of audio and video is low.  Video is good for showing 

>surface appearances of things but lousy at analysis.  It can't crack the 

>surface.  


Not necessarily. An example - A book on fly fishing can describe the
motions necessary to make a good cast. It can even describe the physics
involved in great detail. But forty hours of reading will not prepare
you to fly cast as well as twenty minutes with an expert angler. By
watching him, listening to the rhythm of his cast, watching where he puts
the line, and feeling the heft of your rod, I bet you will gain more useful
knowledge and retain it better than anything you ever read on the subject.

Another example - I want any surgeon that is going to operate on me to
have a heavy background in the specialty, and certainly that will include
huge amounts of textually-transmitted knowledge (in the present anyway).
But if you give me a choice between a doctor who has read every book on
the subject of surgery and one who has read few books, but spent the same
amount of time under the tutelage of a master surgeon, standing next to
him during similar operations and *seeing* him work on the human body and
*listening* to what he is saying and how he is saying it, guess which one
*I* will pick to cut into me with knife or laser (a *very* non-textural
experience!).

>Audio is ephemeral.  It is hard to hold in one place for 

>consideration.

Not anymore. New technologies destroy old limitations. Sight and sound
can now endure for decades. There's no reason to believe that they won't
endure for centuries. Right now, its a bit expensive for an individual or
small group to record experience visually or aurally. But it's getting
cheaper all the time. Soon the cost will be negligible and the limiting
factor will not be the recording, but the content. In the past writing
was the best way to insure that some piece of information you possessed
carried on to others in different places or later in time. It was cheap,
it was easy. But new technologies are becoming cheap and easy as well.

>I am frequently asked asked how I know so much and the answer is always 

>the same.  I read it.

Successful business people are often asked why they are so successful
and they often respond that they *listen* to their customers. To some
extent they mean this figuratively, but having seen several extremely
successful people go through a typical day, I believe that they get the
majority of useful information from actually looking and listening to the
people they encounter.

Writing is great. Reading is awesome. I don't think our species will
ever give this technology up completely - there is a beauty to the
written word which is unique. But many of the reasons for this technology
are disappearing as we approach cybergnosis.

 - jeff

