Date: Wed, 15 Dec 1993 10:34:43 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Hundreds of Uses for Newtons

Derek Zahn writes:

> True, it would require an ongoing effort, but I imagine that TV
> stations are happy to give information out about their upcoming
> programming.  I also imagine that the VCR+ codes are rather

But probably not (yet) in a machine-readable form. Or at least there
will be many formats. Such schedules are coming, but not yet.

> simple to figure out (it has to be a fairly simple coding
> scheme and given a bunch of examples shouldn't be very difficult
> for a clever person [or a GP engine] to nail down).

The VCR+ algorithm was broken, as I said. The catch is that the owners
are claiming certain rights and threatening those who try to build
competing products, and the courts will likely back them (similar to
satellite dish scrambling: breaking the scrambling algorithm does not
entitle one to sell systems which use this).

Derek cites some possible uses of Newton-type gadgets. Many of these
uses are already possible and are in fact being done:

> * What did I do today?  A convenient way to help with time management
>   by recording how time was actually spent.  Similar DayTimer-style
>   apps are of course obvious.

This is mostly built-in to the core Newton already. For more features,
a couple of detailed time management and client billing apps are being
sold. (The Calendar/To Do List feature is very easy to use, with some
clever ways of showing several days at a time, a week at a time, and
so on.)

> * Maps and navigation

Also offered. Cards that plug in showing city maps, restaurants,
hotels, airports, etc. Major cities in the U.S. and Europe. I believe
some of them have tie-ins to the phone dialer, etc., to make hotel
reservations, dial for dinner reservations, and whatnot.

There's some discussion of GPS navigation cards that would fit in the
PCMCIA socket (perhaps with an external protrusion part, at least for
the antenna).

> * Better's apprentice; sports info for the gambler, perhaps even with
>   regression programs built in (don't go to the track without it!)

Steven Levy reports in the latest Macworld magazine that baseball
fanatics can soon buy a card that receives wireless transmissions of
baseball scores in realtime! (One assumes other services, such as
horse races, stock markets, world events, etc., are possible.)

But I think such "better's apprentices" represent a valid niche for
developers. 

> * Shopping list manager.  Whenever you notice something you need to buy,
>   enter it.  Then when at the store just zip out that Newt and never
>   forget to buy mung beans again!

Yep, I already use mine this way. Anything I need I add to my "To Do"
list, with a suitable priority, and then it carries forward from day
to day until I check it "Done."

> * Phone book w phone tone generator.

Already built in, the tone genertor part, at least. From anywhere in
the Newton, write "Call Derek" and tap "Assist." The Newton uses "AI"
(ha!) to try to interpret this, looking at all the "Dereks" and then
asking if it should dial Derek Zahn. Hold the phone up to the Newton's
built-in speaker, and away it goes.

I have every phone number I can think of now in my Newton. (It's also
clever enough to know when to put a "1" in front of the number, etc.,
by using its home location.)

> * Where is that thing?  Enter locations of interesting things and never
>   forget where you saw something.

I'm starting to use it this way, recording where I filed stuff
(physical stuff, that is).

> * Eliza, for therapy on the bus.

Well, I haven't heard about this one. I am working on a cure for
"Rapture of the Future," though. Unfortunately, my clients are not
allowed to go near any advanced technology while going "cold turkey,"
so it can't be run off the Newton.

> * Games, games, games.

Yeah,  a lot have already appeared. Newton Script, the programming
language, is fairly capable and of course the Newton itself has a 20
MHz RISC processor (the ARM 610), so it's got some serious computer
power. Most folks I know are eager to see a good implementation of
"Go" on the Newton--it should be ideal for this, what with the screen
resolution, the pen interface, and the compute power.

(I'll pay $100 for a Nemesis- or Goliath-class Go program.)

> * Drawing program for overhead slides and the like; work on presentations
>   while stuck in traffic.

A couple of such "presentation helpers" are already available. And the
built-in graphics can already be easily faxed or transmitted to
existing Macs and Windows systems.

> * Local event calendar.  Some enterprising local homeless person gets the
>   papers and enters concerts, lectures, etc for downloading by busy
>   yuppies.

A good idea...the first idea Derek has mentioned that isn't already in
the works or being discussed (based on my knowledge, of course).

> * Workout apprentice (and other stopwatch functions).  Time rowing machine
>   regimens, enter weight values for various nautilus exercises, keep
>   track of weight.

Haven't seen this one either, and I haven't yet been tempted to take
my Newton into the workout area of my gym. (But then I just do the
machines and don't even write down how many reps I do.)

> * Calorie counter.

I think I saw an app that does this. Complete with conversions for
fat, calories, etc.

> * Baby data -- enter everything that Baby does, for future BlackNet sale.
> 
> * Car data -- gas mileage, oil change points, etc.

Both of these are possible using the built-in apps. Entering lots of
text, though, is not the Newton's best area. I'd  use a camcorder for
the ankle biter.

> * Calendar apps of various kinds.

Built in. Extensive calendar functions.

> * Scanner attachment, OCR in the library.

Yes, Jay Freeman noted the same thing when he saw my Newton. Could be
a good product. Could even be a PCMCIA card (but would need to include
on-oboard memory, I think.)

> * Engineering formulae on-line.

Some of these are built-in, and a full-functioned, multi-moded
calculator called "Go Figure" is for sale (choice of algebraic or RPN
notation abvailable in all modes, on the fly!). A spreadsheet is
already out, and the "MobileMath" program Ray Cromwell mentioned
should be shipped to me next week (Thanks to Ray for mentiontioning
the free offer!).

> * Bible verses!

Yes, every good Extropian will want this!

> * Speech rehearsing, other memorization needs.
> 
> * Speed reading trainer.
> 
> * Molecular nanotechnology simulations; protein folding.

As Derek is clearly now drifting into Rapture of the Future, I'll
stop. 

But clearly his enthusiastic ideas tell you why the Newton is now
really gathering some momentum: people see what can be done with a
computer that is truly a "Personal Digital Assistant," that is with
you at all times and in all places.

>From what it can already do now--most things on Derek's list are
supported either by the built-in capablities of the current model or
by add-on products--one can imagine the capabilities of
next-generation machines.

Amazing possibilities.

(At the Cypherpunks meeting last Saturday we also waxed rhapsodic
about possibilites for key exchange, PGP on the Newton, digital money
"beamed: through the IR port, and the Newton as a harbinger of future
"carry everywhere" truly personal computers.)

