Arthur Hlavaty writes:
> 	To me, being "fully human" means having the entire range of
> human experience, including death.  No, thanks.

Well, I suppose you could look at it that way, but I was thinking more
along the lines of Nitschze's Ubermensch. There are all sorts of
powers, abilities, knowledge, and heroism available to a human. Most
people don't approach even a small fraction of them.

Sure, I eventually want to hold worlds in my hand and play with them
like marbles. But until I've acheived those powers, I think it unwise
to forsake the meager powers I have at present, because they're only
meager by comparison.

It's unhealthy, in my opinion, to want godhead because humanity seems
too limited. Better, I think, to walk the Earth like a god today and
wish for more powers because you like the ones you already have.

